/*
 * Auto-generated FFT test vectors for motor temperature prediction Model
 *
 * Source: Electric Motor Temperature Dataset (measures_v2.csv)
 * Task: Regression - Predict rotor temperature (pm)
 *
 * Model Input: Tensor[(1, 11, 62, 11), float32] - FFT features
 * Output: Tensor[(1, 1), float32] - Predicted rotor temperature (degrees C)
 *
 * FFT Transformation Parameters:
 *   - frame_size: 128 samples per frame
 *   - num_frames: 32 frames (4096 / 128)
 *   - feature_size_per_frame: 11 frequency bins
 *   - num_frame_concat: 2 frames concatenated
 *   - frame_skip: 1 (sliding window step)
 *   - Output shape: 31 anchors x 2 frames = 62 height, 11 features width
 *
 * FFT Processing Pipeline:
 *   Raw [1, 11, 4096, 1] -> FFT Features [1, 11, 62, 11]
 *   1. Split into 32 frames of 128 samples each
 *   2. Apply Hann window
 *   3. FFT -> Magnitude spectrum (65 bins)
 *   4. Bin aggregation: 11 features (bins 1-22, pairs summed)
 *   5. Log magnitude (dB): 10 * log10(mag)
 *   6. Frame concatenation: 31 sliding positions x 2 frames
 *
 * Channels (11 sensor measurements):
 *   0 - u_q: Voltage q-component
 *   1 - coolant: Coolant temperature
 *   2 - stator_winding: Stator winding temperature
 *   3 - u_d: Voltage d-component
 *   4 - stator_tooth: Stator tooth temperature
 *   5 - motor_speed: Motor speed (RPM)
 *   6 - i_d: Current d-component
 *   7 - i_q: Current q-component
 *   8 - stator_yoke: Stator yoke temperature
 *   9 - ambient: Ambient temperature
 *  10 - torque: Motor torque
 *
 * Test vectors at different operating conditions:
 *   LOW_TEMP  - Low temperature operation (profile 3)
 *   MID_TEMP  - Mid temperature operation (profile 17)
 *   HIGH_TEMP - High temperature operation (profile 19)
 *
 * Usage:
 *   Define MOTOR_TEMP_LOW_TEMP, MOTOR_TEMP_MID_TEMP, or MOTOR_TEMP_HIGH_TEMP
 *   Then use if_map as input tensor for Model inference.
 *   Compare output against MOTOR_TEMP_EXPECTED
 */

